          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>War of the Seven Years</strong><br>\nThe Seven Years' War (1756-1763) was a defining moment in Frederick's reign. Despite being outnumbered, Frederick's military brilliance allowed Prussia to emerge as a significant European power. His leadership during this time earned him respect and the title \"Frederick the Great.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Late Reign and Legacy</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Frederick continued to consolidate Prussia's power and worked on the beautification of Berlin. He became known as an 'enlightened despot' for his reforms and contributions to the state. His legacy includes the promotion of Prussian efficiency and military prestige, which persisted long after his death in 1786.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Succession</strong><br>\nFrederick the Great died in 1786 after a reign of 46 years. He left no heirs, which led to the succession by his nephew Frederick William II. Frederick the Great's death marked the end of an era, but his military and cultural legacy continued to influence European history for generations.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
